  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14108364",
    "id": "q-37982",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-37982",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9cc25edf-9d32-5b47-ae4f-e370d2d0d024",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Points at Which Tangent Planes Have Particular Properties",
      "problem": "At which points on the surface $z=3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ is the tangent plane perpendicular to the vector  $v\\,=\\,⟨3,5,-1⟩?$",
      "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) \\\\  & =3x-\\frac{1}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨3,5,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨3y,\\,3x-\\frac{1}{y},\\,-1⟩=⟨3,5,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3cy=3 \\\\ 3cx-\\frac{c}{y}=5 \\\\ -c=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=1.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3(1)y=3\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=1$ and $y=1$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n3(1)x-\\frac{1}{1}=5\\,⟹\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=2$ and $y=1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =3xy-\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\  & =3(2)(1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector  $v$ at $(2,1,6).$",
